# Authenticated localhost profile file operations

## Scope and existing evidence

Expose caller-authorized load/save for versioned notebook and handwriting
profiles through the existing loopback-only Rust runtime.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Acceptance decision

Completed — practical native Rust and localhost UI integration on
November 3, 2026.

Starting the existing Rust runtime with `--profiles-dir EXISTING_DIRECTORY`
explicitly selects a bounded local profile folder. The authenticated browser
notebook and handwriting editors expose separate open/save actions for versioned
native profiles, without persistent browser state or a second application
engine.

The Rust runtime exposes GET/POST on `/api/notebook/file` and
`/api/handwriting/file`; POST requires the current bearer,
same-origin admission,
a single simple JSON basename, and either a create-only marker or exact source
SHA-256 revision. Native adapters reuse bounded reads, canonical validation
and Linux atomic file commits.

Accepted example: the focused Rust integration test created a notebook file,
reopened it, edited page-number preference and saved with a known revision;
handwriting profiles independently round-tripped and retained their namespace.
Browser mock tests exercised open, revise, save and returned revision on both
local editors. A valid HTTP POST through the production router and real store
replaced the observed notebook file on disk.

Refused examples: the integrated HTTP path returned 401 for an unauthorized
notebook POST and 409 for a stale revision; both retained byte-identical disk
contents. Real file tests also refused symlinks, unknown paths, malformed
profiles and create collisions.

Checks performed: 59 Rust runtime-bootstrap tests and 104 Rust session-runtime
tests passed; frontend typecheck and build passed, focused browser editors
passed and 70 browser privacy/security regression tests passed. Full browser
Firefox suite did not complete successfully owing to prolonged Firefox test
failures under concurrent runs; no complete browser E2E pass is claimed.

Known limits: file commit currently Linux-only, no native Windows/macOS file
save guarantee, no cross-process locking or guarantee against an external
writer racing the last revision check, and no handwriting-stroke rendering.
Historical academic evidence remains not independently reverified.
